/* data/detail-c/phy-11-06.js - GENERATED by tools/build.py from data/detail/phy-11-06.json (our own words). Do not edit. */
(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["phy-11-06"] = {"phy-11-06-m01":{"idea":"Heat and temperature are different things. Heat is energy that moves; temperature tells how hot a body is.","steps":["Heat is the energy that passes from one body to another because they are at different temperatures.","Energy flows from the hotter body to the colder body until both reach the same temperature.","So heat is energy in transit, and not something a body holds inside."],"others":"Average molecular speed is related to temperature, not to heat. Heat is not a substance stored in a body. Temperature is measured in kelvin, but it is not the same as heat.","trap":"Do not say a body 'contains heat'. A body contains internal energy; heat is the part that flows.","answer":"energy that flows from a hotter body to a colder one"},"phy-11-06-m02":{"idea":"The direction of heat flow depends only on temperature, not on how much energy a body has.","steps":["Heat flows from the body at the higher temperature to the body at the lower temperature.","A big cold lake has more total energy than a cup of hot tea.","Even so, heat goes from the tea to the lake, because the tea is hotter."],"others":"A larger quantity of heat does not decide the direction. Specific heat and mass change how much heat is needed, but not which way it flows.","trap":"Think of temperature as 'pressure' for heat. It sets the direction, like a height difference sets the flow of water.","answer":"a higher temperature than the second"},"phy-11-06-m03":{"idea":"The ice point and the steam point are the two fixed points of the Celsius scale.","steps":["On the Celsius scale, pure ice melts at 0 °C.","Water boils at normal pressure at 100 °C.","So the two fixed points are 0 °C and 100 °C, with 100 equal divisions between them."],"others":"32 °C and 100 °C mixes the Fahrenheit ice point with the Celsius steam point. 273 and 373 are the same points in kelvin, not in °C. 0 °C and 212 °C mixes the two scales.","trap":"Remember the pairs: Celsius 0 and 100, Fahrenheit 32 and 212, Kelvin 273 and 373.","answer":"0 °C and 100 °C"},"phy-11-06-m04":{"idea":"Two temperature scales that both have fixed points are linked by a simple proportion.","steps":["Celsius has 100 divisions between the ice and steam points.","Fahrenheit has 180 divisions between its fixed points (32 °F to 212 °F).","The same reading counted from the ice point gives C/100 = (F − 32)/180."],"others":"Using F + 32 puts the zero in the wrong place. C/100 = F/180 forgets that Fahrenheit starts at 32. C/180 = (F − 32)/100 swaps the two scale sizes.","trap":"Subtract 32 from F first, because Fahrenheit starts at 32 at the ice point.","answer":"C/100 = (F − 32)/180"},"phy-11-06-m05":{"idea":"To find where two scales read the same number, put the same value in the conversion formula.","steps":["Let the common reading be x, so C = F = x.","Put it in C/100 = (F − 32)/180 to get x/100 = (x − 32)/180.","Cross-multiply: 180x = 100x − 3200.","So 80x = −3200 and x = −40."],"others":"100 and 0 are the boiling and freezing points in Celsius, and they read different on Fahrenheit. 32 °F is 0 °C, not equal numbers.","trap":"The answer is negative. Only at −40 do the two scales agree.","answer":"−40"},"phy-11-06-m06":{"idea":"Absolute zero is the lowest possible temperature and the zero of the Kelvin scale.","steps":["The volume of an ideal gas at constant pressure falls in a straight line as it cools.","The line reaches zero volume at −273.15 °C.","This point is taken as 0 K, so T(K) = t(°C) + 273.15."],"others":"The second option swaps the scales: kelvin cannot be negative. The third gives 0 °C as absolute zero, but that is the freezing point of water. The fourth gives a made-up value.","trap":"Kelvin has no negative values.","answer":"−273.15 °C, or 0 K"},"phy-11-06-m07":{"idea":"Gas laws use absolute temperature because gas pressure and volume are proportional to it.","steps":["In PV = nRT, the temperature T is on one side and P, V on the other.","If T were in Celsius, a gas at 0 °C would have zero pressure or volume, which is wrong.","So T must be in kelvin, where zero means no thermal motion."],"others":"Any scale would not work, because the laws need a true zero. Fahrenheit and Celsius both have arbitrary zeros.","trap":"Always add 273 (or 273.15) to Celsius before using a gas law.","answer":"in kelvin, as the pressure and volume are proportional to it"},"phy-11-06-m08":{"idea":"At constant volume, the pressure of a gas is proportional to its absolute temperature (Gay-Lussac's law).","steps":["At constant volume, P ∝ T when T is in kelvin.","The temperature goes from 300 K to 600 K, so it doubles.","Therefore the pressure also doubles."],"others":"Four times would follow if the pressure went with the square of T. Unchanged and half do not match a heated gas at fixed volume.","trap":"If the temperatures were in Celsius, say 27 °C to 327 °C, you must first change them to kelvin.","answer":"twice the original"},"phy-11-06-m09":{"idea":"The ideal gas equation joins pressure, volume, amount and temperature in one relation.","steps":["Boyle's, Charles' and Avogadro's laws together give PV ∝ nT.","Adding the constant R gives PV = nRT.","Here R = 8.314 J/(mol K), and T is in kelvin."],"others":"The other three forms put the variables in the wrong places, so their units do not match: for example PT = nRV does not follow from the gas laws.","trap":"Use absolute pressure and kelvin temperature in PV = nRT.","answer":"PV = nRT"},"phy-11-06-m10":{"idea":"The coefficient of linear expansion tells how much a length grows, as a fraction, for each degree of heating.","steps":["The increase in length is ΔL.","The fractional increase is ΔL/L.","Per unit temperature rise, this is α = ΔL/(L ΔT).","Its unit is per kelvin (K⁻¹)."],"others":"ΔL/ΔT is the growth per degree, but it does not divide by the original length L. The other two forms have the wrong dimensions.","trap":"α is per unit length, so L is in the denominator.","answer":"α = ΔL/(L ΔT)"},"phy-11-06-m11":{"idea":"Expansion in length, area and volume follow from the same small change in each dimension.","steps":["Each length grows by the fraction αΔT.","Area is length × breadth, so it grows by about 2αΔT, which gives β = 2α.","Volume has three dimensions, so it grows by about 3αΔT, which gives γ = 3α.","So α : β : γ = 1 : 2 : 3."],"others":"1 : 3 : 2 puts area and volume the wrong way round. 3 : 2 : 1 reverses the whole order. 1 : 1 : 1 would mean that volume does not grow more than length.","trap":"These ratios hold only for small temperature changes.","answer":"α : β : γ = 1 : 2 : 3"},"phy-11-06-m12":{"idea":"A hole in a heated plate behaves as if it were made of the same metal as the plate.","steps":["Imagine the hole filled with a disc of the same metal.","Heat the plate: the disc and the plate would expand by the same fraction.","So the hole also expands, and its diameter increases."],"others":"The metal does not expand into the hole, because all lengths on the plate grow together. The diameter cannot stay the same or go to zero.","trap":"Everyone expects the hole to shrink. It does not.","answer":"increases, as the hole expands just like the material around it"},"phy-11-06-m13":{"idea":"Specific heat capacity is the heat needed per unit mass for one degree of temperature rise.","steps":["The heat for a temperature change is Q = m s ΔT.","If m = 1 kg and ΔT = 1 K, then Q = s.","So s is the heat needed to raise 1 kg by 1 K."],"others":"Changing a state at a fixed temperature is latent heat, not specific heat. Raising 1 kg from 0 °C to 100 °C is a different amount. 'The whole body' describes heat capacity, not specific heat.","trap":"Specific heat is per kilogram; heat capacity is for the whole body.","answer":"raise the temperature of 1 kg of it by 1 K"},"phy-11-06-m14":{"idea":"Calorimetry is conservation of energy: heat lost by hot bodies equals heat gained by cold bodies.","steps":["Put the hot metal in cold water inside an insulated calorimeter.","No heat escapes, so the energy lost by the hot body is gained by the water and the vessel.","So heat lost = heat gained."],"others":"The final temperature is the average only when the heat capacities are equal. Equal masses are not required. Temperature is not a conserved quantity, only heat is.","trap":"Equating temperature changes is wrong. Equate heat, m s ΔT.","answer":"the heat lost by the hot body equals the heat gained by the cold bodies"},"phy-11-06-m15":{"idea":"During a change of state, the heat goes into changing the arrangement of molecules, not into raising the temperature.","steps":["While ice melts at 0 °C, heat keeps being supplied.","The energy breaks the bonds between molecules; it does not make them move faster.","So the temperature stays constant until all the ice has melted."],"others":"The heat is absorbed, so it does not disappear. The temperature does not drop and does not rise during the change.","trap":"A flat part on a heating graph means a change of state.","answer":"does not change the temperature, but is used to change the state"},"phy-11-06-m16":{"idea":"Latent heat is the heat taken in or given out at constant temperature during a change of state.","steps":["Changing a solid to a liquid at its melting point needs heat.","The heat per kilogram for this change is the latent heat of fusion.","The heat needed is Q = m L, where L is the latent heat."],"others":"Specific heat capacity is for a temperature change. Heat capacity is for a whole body. Latent heat of vaporisation is for liquid to gas.","trap":"Fusion is solid to liquid; vaporisation is liquid to gas.","answer":"latent heat of fusion"},"phy-11-06-m17":{"idea":"Vaporisation needs far more energy than melting because the molecules go much farther apart.","steps":["In melting, molecules stay close and only the fixed pattern is loosened.","In boiling, the molecules are separated completely.","The gas also pushes the air back as it expands, which is extra work.","So the latent heat of vaporisation is much larger."],"others":"The mass does not change in either process. The temperature is not the reason. The option about fusion describes vaporisation, not melting.","trap":"Water: fusion 3.33 × 10⁵ J/kg, vaporisation 22.6 × 10⁵ J/kg.","answer":"in vaporisation, the molecules must be separated completely, and the gas pushes the air back"},"phy-11-06-m18":{"idea":"Only radiation can carry heat through empty space.","steps":["Conduction needs a material to pass energy along.","Convection needs a fluid that can move.","Space between the Sun and Earth is almost empty.","So the heat reaches us as radiation, in electromagnetic waves."],"others":"Conduction and convection both need matter, so neither can cross a vacuum.","trap":"Only radiation works without a medium.","answer":"radiation, which needs no medium"},"phy-11-06-m19":{"idea":"In a solid rod, heat passes along by conduction, without the material itself moving.","steps":["One end of the metal rod is heated.","The molecules there vibrate more and pass energy to their neighbours.","The molecules stay in place, and only energy moves along the rod.","That is conduction."],"others":"Convection needs the matter itself to move, which a solid cannot do. Radiation is by waves, and it is not the main way along a metal rod.","trap":"Conduction: energy moves, matter does not. Convection: matter moves with the energy.","answer":"conduction, without any movement of the matter"},"phy-11-06-m20":{"idea":"Conduction rate grows with area and temperature difference, and falls with length.","steps":["More area gives more paths for heat, so the rate ∝ A.","A larger temperature difference pushes more heat, so the rate ∝ (T₁ − T₂).","A longer rod gives a smaller gradient, so the rate ∝ 1/x.","Together: rate = kA(T₁ − T₂)/x."],"others":"Multiplying by x would make a longer rod conduct more, which is wrong. k(T₁ − T₂)/(Ax) divides by the area. kAx/(T₁ − T₂) turns the temperature difference upside down.","trap":"Length is in the denominator, area is in the numerator.","answer":"kA(T₁ − T₂)/x, where k is the thermal conductivity"},"phy-11-06-m21":{"idea":"For the same temperatures and area, the heat flow is inversely proportional to the length of the rod.","steps":["Rate = kA(T₁ − T₂)/L.","Only L changes, and it becomes 2L.","So the rate becomes half."],"others":"Double or four times would need the length in the numerator or squared. Staying the same ignores the length.","trap":"A longer rod conducts less, not more.","answer":"becomes half"},"phy-11-06-m22":{"idea":"A body cools faster when it is much hotter than its surroundings.","steps":["Newton's law of cooling says the rate of heat loss depends on the temperature difference.","Written out: |dT/dt| = C (T − T₀), where T₀ is the temperature of the surroundings.","So the rate is proportional to T − T₀."],"others":"The rate does not depend on the absolute temperature alone, or on the mass alone, or on the square of the surroundings' temperature.","trap":"The law works for small temperature differences.","answer":"the difference between the temperature of the body and that of its surroundings"},"phy-11-06-m23":{"idea":"In Newton's cooling, the excess over the room falls by the same factor in equal times.","steps":["Excess at the start: 80 − 20 = 60 °C.","After 5 minutes: 60 − 20 = 40 °C, so the factor is 40/60 = 2/3.","After another 5 minutes: 40 × 2/3 = 26.7 °C.","So T = 20 + 26.7 = 46.7 °C."],"others":"40 °C would assume it falls by 20 degrees again, but cooling slows as the difference shrinks. 50 °C and 43.3 °C come from wrong ratios.","trap":"Cooling is not linear in time. It follows a ratio.","answer":"about 46.7 °C"},"phy-11-06-m24":{"idea":"A heating graph is flat while a substance changes state.","steps":["Before melting, the solid warms and the graph rises.","During melting, the heat is used as latent heat and the temperature stays constant.","So the graph is flat during melting.","After melting, the liquid warms and the graph rises again."],"others":"A falling curve does not occur while heating. A vertical line would mean a sudden jump in temperature. A rising line is true before and after, but not during melting.","trap":"The slopes before and after are different because specific heats differ.","answer":"flat, as the temperature stays constant while the latent heat is absorbed"},"phy-11-06-n01":{"idea":"Change Celsius to Fahrenheit with F = (9/5)C + 32.","steps":["Use F = (9/5)C + 32.","Put C = 37: (9/5) × 37 = 66.6.","F = 66.6 + 32 = 98.6 °F."],"trap":"Check with C/100 = (F − 32)/180: 0.37 × 180 = 66.6, so F − 32 = 66.6.","answer":"98.6 °F"},"phy-11-06-n02":{"idea":"A gas thermometer assumes pressure changes in a straight line with temperature.","steps":["Pressure at 0 °C is 100 kPa; at 100 °C it is 137 kPa.","So 37 kPa corresponds to 100 °C, which is 0.37 kPa per degree.","A reading of 120 kPa is 20 kPa above the ice point.","T = 100 × 20/37 = 54.05 °C."],"trap":"Always measure the reading from the ice-point value, not from zero.","answer":"54.05 °C"},"phy-11-06-n03":{"idea":"Find pressure from the ideal gas equation PV = nRT.","steps":["P = nRT/V.","n = 2.0 mol, R = 8.314 J/(mol K), T = 300 K, V = 0.050 m³.","P = 2.0 × 8.314 × 300/0.050 = 99 768 Pa, which is about 9.98 × 10⁴ Pa.","In kPa: 99.8 kPa."],"trap":"Check: one mole at 273 K and 1 atm takes 22.4 L, so 2 mol in 50 L at 300 K should be a little under 1 atm.","answer":"99.8 kPa"},"phy-11-06-n04":{"idea":"A solid grows in length in proportion to its length and the temperature rise.","steps":["ΔL = α L ΔT.","ΔT = 40 − 10 = 30 °C.","ΔL = 1.2 × 10⁻⁵ × 20 × 30 = 7.2 × 10⁻³ m.","In mm: 7.2 mm."],"trap":"That is why railway tracks have small gaps.","answer":"7.2 mm"},"phy-11-06-n05":{"idea":"Volume expansion uses γ = 3α for a solid.","steps":["γ = 3α = 3 × 1.1 × 10⁻⁵ = 3.3 × 10⁻⁵ per °C.","ΔV = γ V ΔT.","ΔV = 3.3 × 10⁻⁵ × 1000 × 100 = 3.3 cm³.","To 2 decimal places: 3.30 cm³."],"trap":"Do not use α directly for volume; use γ = 3α.","answer":"3.30 cm³"},"phy-11-06-n06":{"idea":"Mixing hot and cold water: heat lost equals heat gained.","steps":["The specific heat is the same, so it cancels. Use mass × temperature change.","Hot water loses 200 (80 − T), cold water gains 300 (T − 20).","200 (80 − T) = 300 (T − 20), so 16 000 − 200T = 300T − 6000.","500T = 22 000, so T = 44.0 °C."],"trap":"The answer lies between 20 °C and 80 °C, closer to 20 °C because there is more cold water.","answer":"44.0 °C"},"phy-11-06-n07":{"idea":"Heating ice to water has three stages: warm the ice, melt it, warm the water.","steps":["Warm ice from −10 °C to 0 °C: 0.1 × 2100 × 10 = 2100 J.","Melt the ice at 0 °C: 0.1 × 3.33 × 10⁵ = 33 300 J.","Warm the water from 0 °C to 20 °C: 0.1 × 4200 × 20 = 8400 J.","Total = 2100 + 33 300 + 8400 = 43 800 J."],"trap":"The melting step is the largest. Do not skip the latent heat.","answer":"43800 J"},"phy-11-06-n08":{"idea":"Steady heat conduction along a rod uses P = kA(T₁ − T₂)/x.","steps":["k = 400 W/(m K), A = 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ m², ΔT = 100 K, x = 0.50 m.","P = 400 × 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ × 100/0.50.","400 × 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ = 0.16, and 0.16 × 100 = 16.","16/0.50 = 32.0 W."],"trap":"Check units: W/(m K) × m² × K / m = W.","answer":"32.0 W"},"phy-11-06-n09":{"idea":"In Newton's law of cooling, the excess temperature falls by a fixed factor in equal times.","steps":["Excess at the start: 80 − 20 = 60 °C. After 5 min: 60 − 20 = 40 °C.","The factor per 5 minutes is 40/60 = 2/3.","After the next 5 minutes: 40 × 2/3 = 26.7 °C.","T = 20 + 26.7 = 46.7 °C."],"trap":"Cooling does not fall by 20 degrees every 5 minutes.","answer":"46.7 °C"},"phy-11-06-n10":{"idea":"Use the principle of calorimetry: heat lost by the metal equals heat gained by the water.","steps":["Metal loses heat: 0.50 × s × (100 − 30) = 0.50 × s × 70 = 35 s.","Water gains heat: 0.40 × 4200 × (30 − 20) = 16 800 J.","35 s = 16 800.","s = 16 800/35 = 480 J/(kg K)."],"trap":"Check: 0.50 × 480 × 70 = 16 800 J, the same as the water gained.","answer":"480 J/(kg K)"},"phy-11-06-n11":{"idea":"At constant volume, P/T stays the same, with T in kelvin.","steps":["T₁ = 27 + 273.15 = 300.15 K and T₂ = 127 + 273.15 = 400.15 K.","P₂/P₁ = T₂/T₁ = 400.15/300.15.","P₂ = 1.000 × 1.3332 = 1.333 atm."],"trap":"Using Celsius would give 127/27, which is wrong.","answer":"1.333 atm"}};
